    "q-12570": {
      "dom_id": "step-q12570",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{log}_{3}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{3}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{log}_{3}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})-\\int (-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{3}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-12570-a",
